let's bring in Dan Shaughnessy does a  great job covering baseball for the  Boston Globe baseball hall-of-famer Dan  is it a broadcasters responsibility to  be in the clubhouse as David Price  suggests I think it doesn't hurt but I  don't think it's a required especially  when you spend twenty four years in the  big leagues in clubhouse tennis actually  has I mean this is a phony argument by  Christ I'm not buying it I mean I'm in  the clubhouse all the time they don't  want you there and I understand why in  many cases but you know the notion is  they expect X players to be good to  players and when it doesn't happen and  again it's not been highly critical  about 10 seconds commentary it's very  blunt and candid and he says a million  good things when good things happen if  somebody doesn't do the job he'll call  him on it same as he'll call it on  himself this was the most stand-up guy I  think I ever covered in the big leagues  go back and look at what he did after he  gave up the gibson home run the one in  the dodger you know world series 1988 he  took wave after wave of reporters he  never made excuses he deflected  criticism of other guys took it on  himself and it's too bad ugly for David  Price too you know he's also told us  some people don't understand how hard  this game is that is so upside down it  should be on the Bale what do you think  is that the root of prices issues with  Eckersley is it one particular comment  is it a combination of comments I think  there's an overall feeling in that room  that he's too critical because you know  Remy and rice are not as critical Tim we  have a lot of xx ox guys who give  commentary and they just expect players  to say all the things and cover up their  mistakes and I think that's not what  you're going to get with Eckersley and  that's the fans like that it's better  that way so it doesn't wash well with  the players necessarily and I think that  David's just doesn't like the way he  gets talked about himself and it's using  this notion that he's standing up for  his teammates nobody asked him to stand  up for them and to try to embarrass a  guy hall-of-famer on a plane like that  and then to not you know to not have the  manager or other teammates say hey  that's not the way we do things here  which would be the correct way to handle  this that hasn't been done it's the only  reason they turn a one-day story into a  1-month story and why do you think that  the Red Sox particularly John Farrell  have taken sort of in  ambiguous position and bibble insane  this issue feral as as said he has not  spoken Eckersley he has not apologized  to Eckersley why do you think the Red  Sox er are taking this approach John  Farrell's either afraid the player or he  agrees with them either case it's not  good now above Farrell there's been all  kinds of apologies you know the owners  Sam Kennedy the CEO Dave Dombrowski can  all said the right thing not going to do  things here but they don't seem to have  the power over their two hundred and  seventeen million dollar picture to make  that happen so again the thing is still  sitting there and fans are pretty much a  hundred percent behind Eckersley and  zero behind price which is not good the  teams lost 14 out of 22 they don't need  this distraction players are getting  asked to applaud and who didn't you  notice it's become way way bigger one  stupid thing has become way bigger they  could have made it all go away  so I had an Shaughnessy Boston Globe  colonist joining us here on the Dan  Patrick show how does this get resolved  or doesn't get resolved I mean Eckersley  was in Cooperstown over the weekend is  he back on the job  this week do you think of any lingering  issues moving forward well equities in  what they call the legend suite at  Fenway tomorrow it's a ceremonial thing  they'll put Dwight Evans and bring guys  back and give the night with fans in the  suite upstairs and they they show on the  big board during the game when that  happens tomorrow will be like Bill  Belichick and Brady or they're on the  big board and some probably a nice  reception he's back on the booth on  Thursday it was talked about a meeting  with price and Eckersley I don't think X  isn't supposed to meet with a guy who is  thus far not apologetic still calling  him out for not being in the clubhouse  and basically clueless about the whole  situation one of the things that I  wonder when this person went down Dan  was you know there was a vacuum kind of  left behind when David Ortiz left that  clubhouse do you think this would be  happening if Ortiz was around I do not I  mean lt's was at the ballpark yesterday  Davis done a nice job staying clear this  year because you know he doesn't want to  be a distraction he has not been he came  by yesterday to help on of the 2007  champs he was part of that and in the  course of it was asked a lot of  questions and and said yeah he said he  likes everybody but he said he had dated  apologized at the end part of all this  is that you know David Price is hurt on  the DL with the the elbow problems how  significant are these elbow issues for  rice yeah that's really the larger  baseball story here and that's what they  should be talking about now right now  it's you know he didn't pitch in spring  training took two months off he's never  had elbow trouble led the nature's  meetings last year so there was concern  you know 31 years old never he really  had an issue there and all the money  still owe to him so they went slowly he  didn't go for Tommy John that seemed  good and then when he made his debut he  got better each time or command so they  had that great outing eight innings of  shutout ball against the Yanks on  national TV a couple of Sundays ago then  he had a stinker in Anaheim on a late  Saturday kind of got lost and and then  he was blocking on him he said and they  put him on the deal before his start at  home last Friday so they're saying he's  going to pick up a ball in a day or two  and give it a shot and then I think they  can pitch him later in the homestand or  if it's bad in the back Andrews and  explore right now they're trying to  minimize the extent of this and they're  going to need him to get where they want  to go the last two months  John Farrell in any trouble out there  well wouldn't be good they'll make a  playoff so if they got broomed out in  the first round again like they did last  year I think that would be bad for him  but they have the best team and then I  play in that way so that's never a good  thing it's five years here he's got a  chance to finish first or the third time  in five years that would be pretty good  and that would give him quite a bit of  solidarity you would think but they need  to do better in October than they did  last year hey always great to visit with  you Dan  appreciate the time anytime take care  Stan Shana covered the Red Sox at the  Boston Globe the Dan Patrick show  weekday mornings on audience

